Subject: [PATCH 0021] Capture a new-game start state instead of a DEMO1 start state

Every retained start state is the image the account-backed driver holds
right after G_DoPlayDemo(): the DEMO1 header chose E1M1 and its recorded
skill, and the recorded player (health, armour and angle already spent
one demo tic of input) is the start.  A play demo wants the other thing:
the state G_InitNew(sk_hard, 1, 1) leaves, which is what a player gets
for pressing "new game".

G_DoPlayDemo and G_InitNew converge on the same engine.  G_DoPlayDemo
reads skill, episode, map, consoleplayer and playeringame[] out of the
13-byte demo header and then calls G_InitNew, which calls G_DoLoadLevel.
The only things G_DoPlayDemo contributes are the demo's own values, so a
new-game start state needs no engine change at all, only a different
caller.

--new-game SKILL selects the G_InitNew path in place of G_DoPlayDemo: it
sets the three values G_InitNew's vanilla comment requires the caller to
supply (a zeroed players[] array, playeringame[0] and consoleplayer 0 --
the demo header supplies them today), calls G_InitNew(skill, 1, 1), and
leaves demo_p on the first DEMO1 ticcmd slot so the ST1 header's
demo-cursor field is the 13 the demo capture carries.  The demo lump is
still cached PU_STATIC, so DoomControllerSetCapturedDemoBounds still has
its real bounds and DOOM-SIM/2's cursor validation is unchanged.

--new-game-precache keeps precache at its D_DoomMain value across
G_InitNew; without it precache is cleared for the load, which is what
G_DoPlayDemo does.  Measured on this source the two captures are
byte-identical, because precache only selects R_PrecacheLevel()
(p_setup.c:664) and that touches no thinker, no mobj and no ST1 field.
The flag is kept because the demo path clears precache and a capture
should record what the engine did rather than what it skipped, and
because that is a property of this revision, not of the format.

This patch touches only the headless driver.  g_game.c, p_setup.c and
every translation unit the SBF image folds in are untouched, so no
program behaviour changes and the built image is unaffected.

Applies on top of 0001-0020.

